Subject: adds an experimental register-bytecode backend and interpreter, guarded by QJS_REGVM

What this does
  Compiles each function's final stack bytecode into a register form when the
  function is created, and runs that form in a register interpreter whose
  operands name a frame-resident register file instead of an operand stack.
  A function the backend cannot lower keeps no register program and fails closed
  rather than falling back to the stack interpreter.

  The parser and the stack bytecode emitter are unchanged. The register form is
  built from the final stack bytecode after resolve_labels() and stored beside
  it in the function; it is serialized only for the precompiled built-in
  modules, under a tag of its own.

Why we need it
  The operand-stack interpreter pays a frame load and a frame store for every
  local and argument and a push and a pop for every temporary. On stack-shaped
  loops that traffic is most of the work. A register form removes it at the
  source, where a value is read and written in place, and this is the experiment
  that decides whether that is enough to beat the stack VM's per-instruction
  cost on the code react-native-quickjs actually runs.

  Off by default. A build without -DQJS_REGVM compiles none of this and
  produces objects identical to the build before the patch.

What it adds
  quickjs-regvm.h
      The lowering inventory, the register instruction set, the operand
      encoding and one classification line per stack opcode, generated by
      re-including quickjs-opcode.h so an opcode that is not classified is an
      undeclared identifier and fails the build.
  js_regvm_scan() and js_regvm_build_cfg()
      Decode the final bytecode, build a control-flow graph with normal and
      exceptional edges, run an operand-stack SSA to a fixed point with phi
      merges at joins, compute liveness and ownership, and assign physical
      registers by linear scan with phi coalescing and destructive destination
      reuse.
  js_regvm_encode()
      Lower the IR to register bytecode, place the phi edge moves the allocator
      owes, resolve branch targets, and read the bytes back with a bounded
      verifier, refusing a function whole when an instruction has no lowering.
  js_regvm_execute()
      The register interpreter: a computed-goto dispatch loop that executes the
      register bytecode, including the register-native call forms, object
      literals, for-in and for-of, classes, private fields, and the in and
      instanceof operators.
  builtin-*.regvm.h
      The built-in module payloads compiled by the register compiler, so a
      register build is handed a register blob and the reader rejects a stack
      blob before parsing it.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. The register bytecode is a serialized form of its own: a register
  function is written under a dedicated tag, and its body records the register
  format version. A build for the other virtual machine has to reject such an
  artifact before parsing it, so a new tag enters the serialized tag set. The
  version number itself is raised once, in 9999, for the whole series.

Notes
  Guarded by QJS_REGVM, so a stack build compiles none of it. The backend fails
  closed per function: an opcode with no lowering refuses that function, never
  the build.

  Two build switches, both off in a shipping build:

    QJS_REGVM_VERIFY
      The encoder's read-back pass -- re-decode every instruction emitted and
      compare every operand against the IR -- is a check on the compiler, not on
      the program, and is most of the encoder's cost. It is on in a debug build
      (when NDEBUG is not defined) and is asked for explicitly by the gate and
      the ablations.

    QJS_REGVM_KNOBS
      The ~21 REGVM_* environment switches the backend was tuned with. Reading
      them cost 19 M instructions on the box2d compile alone, several were read
      inside per-instruction loops, and an environment variable that changes the
      emitted ISA is a way for two builds of one source to disagree. Without
      this switch every knob folds to NULL.

  This lands the backend and the interpreter; it does not yet win overall. On the
  hosting container (arm64, clang 14, -O3 -DNDEBUG) the register VM executes 71.1
  native instructions per iteration of a bare while loop against the stack VM's
  129.1, and Box2D fixed work executes 65.2 billion instructions against the stack
  VM's 41.7 billion. The interpreter is a function of its own so the dispatch
  loop's live set is not shared with JS_CallInternal's frame, and that alone took
  Box2D from 73.2 to 65.2 billion instructions and its wall time from about 3400
  to about 2970 ms; the two-instruction bare loop is a few percent either way. The
  remaining cost is the register allocator still spilling live values around the
  computed-goto joins, and removing it is the work this patch makes possible.

  A function that uses gosub, ret, nip_catch, with, direct eval, generators or
  async suspension has no lowering and is counted rather than approximated.

